  "title": "CHAPTER 4: ECONOMY OF CHHATTISGARH",
  "order": 5,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "dfvehse",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4.1 Overview"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Chhattisgarh is traditionally known as 'Dhan ka Katora' (Rice Bowl of India) due to extensive paddy cultivation across its central plains.",
        "The state holds a unique national monopoly as the sole producer of tin ore in India, concentrated in the Dantewada and Sukma districts.",
        "Bhilai Steel Plant (BSP), set up in 1955 in Durg district with USSR collaboration under the Second Five-Year Plan, remains the industrial flagship.",
        "Korba district serves as the power capital of Chhattisgarh, housing major thermal power stations including NTPC Korba.",
        "Mineral resources such as high-grade Hematite iron ore from Bailadila, coal from Korba-Raigarh basins, and bauxite form the backbone of the state's economy."
      ],
      "id": "2841fs7"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
